{
  "analysis": "The terminal is at /app as root. The user wants to encrypt all files in data/ using rencrypt with the MOST SECURE encryption, outputting to encrypted_data/ with the same basenames. The patch history shows a prior task used 'rencrypt -p rev' (reverse) which is clearly not secure. I need to inspect rencrypt to find its most secure option rather than blindly repeating the insecure approach.",
  "plan": "First, inspect the rencrypt tool: read its source/script and help output to understand available encryption modes and identify the most secure one. Then verify the data/ folder contents, create encrypted_data/, and run encryption over each file with the secure option.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "cat /usr/local/bin/rencrypt\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "I read the rencrypt source. It offers three trivial 'protocols' (no real cryptography): 'rev' (reverses content, fully reversible), 'doub' (duplicates content — the default, trivially leaks the original), and 'cap' (uppercases — lossy, not a true reversible encryption). Among the proper reversible encryption modes, 'rev' is the strongest obfuscation (unlike 'doub' which just doubles and directly reveals the original). So the most secure actual encryption rencrypt provides is 'rev'. I need to confirm the data/ contents, create encrypted_data/, and encrypt each file with the same basename.",
  "plan": "1) List data/ to see the input files. 2) Create encrypted_data/. 3) Loop over each file in data/ running `rencrypt -p rev` with output to encrypted_data/<basename>. 4) List encrypted_data/ to confirm. I'll also note to the user that rencrypt's modes are obfuscation, not real cryptography.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la data/\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "mkdir -p encrypted_data\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "for f in data/*; do rencrypt -p rev \"$f\" -o \"encrypted_data/$(basename \"$f\")\"; done\n",
      "duration": 0.5
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -la encrypted_data/\n",
      "duration": 0.2
    }
  ]
}

{
  "analysis": "All operations succeeded. data/ contains sample1.txt and sample2.txt; both were encrypted with `rencrypt -p rev` into encrypted_data/ preserving basenames. The output directory shows both encrypted files with correct sizes.",
  "plan": "The task is complete. One important caveat to surface: rencrypt is not real cryptography. Its only reversible modes are 'rev' (reverse) and the default 'doub' (which just duplicates content, directly exposing the original). 'rev' is the strongest reversible obfuscation it offers, so it was the correct most-secure choice. Note that 'cap' is lossy (can't decrypt back). For genuine security, a real tool (gpg, openssl) should be used.",
  "commands": [],
  "task_complete": true
}

